description:
The reconciler releases its publish slot best-effort, and throws the answer away:

    await step.do("release-publish-slot", async () => {
      try {
        await room().releaseSlot(holder);      // return value discarded
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(`run ... could not release the publish slot ...`);
      }
    });

releaseSlot does not THROW on refusal - it RETURNS one:

    export type ReleaseSlotResult =
      | { ok: true; released: DispatchLeaseView }
      | { ok: false; error: "not_holder"; holder: DispatchLeaseView | null; detail: string }
      | RequestInvalid;

So the catch never fires for a refused release. The refusal - which carries the CURRENT holder and a detail string, exactly the two facts a reader needs - is dropped at the point it is produced. A run whose release is refused reports success, leaves the slot held, and says nothing anywhere.

Best-effort is the right POLICY: a failed release must not turn a terminal verdict into a wedged Workflow, and the rooms' alarms sweep what it leaves behind. Best-effort is not a reason to discard the evidence. Log the refusal with its holder and detail, and put it in the run's feed; keep not failing the run.

Observed, not yet explained: during u9h's work a Workflow test asserted the slot was null after a finished run and found one held, with acquired_at and expires_at 62 seconds apart rather than the three-minute TTL - i.e. a RE-ACQUIRED lease. It failed in two different lease-timing tests across two runs and did not reproduce in three runs on either the branch or the base. The cause is NOT established. What is established is that if a refused release is what happened, nothing in the system would have told us - which is why this ticket is worth doing before the cause is known, not after.

Check the sibling: release-dispatch-lease has the same try/catch shape a few lines below; confirm whether releaseDispatchLease throws or returns.
